foxtrot
=======

foxtrot is an SSH-2 engine written in C for the GNU system: the transport
layer (RFC 4253), user authentication (RFC 4252), the connection protocol
(RFC 4254) and SFTP.  It is the substrate on which SSH and SFTP clients and
servers are meant to be built.

It is a sibling of quickstep (QUIC and TLS) and follows the same design: a
thin cryptographic layer over libgcrypt, incremental push parsers that
deliver semantic events, and reactive state machines that never block and
never own a socket.  The library does no I/O.  The caller reads bytes from
its transport and hands them in; the library calls back with the bytes to
write.

Like quickstep it deliberately refuses what is known to be insecure.  The
aim is a complete, conformant, secure-by-design implementation, not one that
is interoperable with every legacy system: no SSH-1, no CBC or CTR
ciphers, no separate MACs, no SHA-1, no finite-field Diffie-Hellman, no
ssh-rsa or DSA, no compression, and Strict KEX (the Terrapin countermeasure)
is mandatory.  See the manual (doc/foxtrot.texi) for the security policy.

Post-quantum key exchange
-------------------------

The default key exchange preference puts the post-quantum hybrids first:
mlkem768x25519-sha256, sntrup761x25519-sha512 (also under its OpenSSH 9.0
name), mlkem768nistp256-sha256 and mlkem1024nistp384-sha384, ahead of
curve25519-sha256 and ECDH P-256/P-384.  Each hybrid runs a classical
elliptic-curve exchange and a post-quantum KEM (ML-KEM or Streamlined NTRU
Prime, from libgcrypt) side by side and hashes the two secrets together, so
the session key is safe if either half holds.  An attacker who records the
traffic now and has a quantum computer later gets nothing from it, and one
who finds a flaw in the newer KEM is no better off than against the curve
alone.  There is deliberately no pure post-quantum method.

Two limits to know.  The classical methods stay in the closed set and are
negotiated with a peer that offers nothing better, so a hybrid is used when
both sides have one, not enforced.  And the hybrid protects confidentiality
only: host and user keys are still Ed25519, ECDSA and RSA, so authenticating
the peer does not resist a quantum computer, and neither do X.509 chains or
OpenSSH certificates.

Certificate validation
----------------------

Two kinds of certificate are supported, and neither is trusted just because
it parses and its signature verifies.  The library checks what it can and
leaves the decision to the application, which has to ask.

OpenSSH certificates (user and host).  gf_key_from_blob refuses one that is
malformed or whose CA signed with SHA-1, or that holds a key or CA key the
policy refuses.  gf_cert_check then tests the type, the validity dates and
the critical options; the CA, the principals and the options are for the
application, as is a revocation list (krl) and known_hosts or authorized_keys
for who may sign what.

X.509 chains (RFC 6187, through GnuTLS).  gf_x509_verify checks the chain
against a trust store the application builds; nothing is trusted by default
and nothing is read from the system.  Every certificate must be in its
dates, the chain must lead to a CA in the store with no MD5 or SHA-1
signature and no key under 112 bits, and none may be on a CRL the store
holds.  Stapled OCSP responses are checked for signature, freshness and
revocation (a stale or broken one fails the chain, and the application can
require one).  The extended key usage must allow secure shell for the role,
the key usage must allow digitalSignature, and a server's subject
alternative names must match the host it was dialled as: the common name is
never consulted.  Nothing is fetched from the network.

Status
------

Pre-alpha.  What exists today:

  wire      the data types of RFC 4251 (string, mpint, name-list)
  policy    the closed set of key exchanges, signatures and ciphers
  ident     the protocol version exchange
  packet    the binary packet protocol: chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com,
            aes256-gcm@openssh.com, aes128-gcm@openssh.com
  kexinit   SSH_MSG_KEXINIT and negotiation; strict key exchange required
  kex       mlkem768x25519, sntrup761x25519, mlkem768nistp256,
            mlkem1024nistp384, curve25519 and ECDH P-256/P-384; exchange
            hash and key derivation
  key       Ed25519, ECDSA P-256/P-384 and RSA host and user keys; OpenSSH
            private key files (also passphrase-protected, with the application's
            bcrypt-pbkdf), public key text, and OpenSSH certificates
            (user and host; CA, dates, principals, source-address,
            force-command; making them, as ssh-keygen -s); FIDO security
            keys (verified by a server; a client reads their key handle and
            packs the answer of a token the application drives)
  keyfmt    other key formats: RFC 4716, PEM/DER (SPKI, PKCS#8 also under
            PBES2, PKCS#1, SEC1), PuTTY .ppk 3, and randomart; read
            from what openssl, ssh-keygen and puttygen write
  x509      X.509 chains as keys (RFC 6187), through GnuTLS: parsing,
            validation against a trust store, CRLs, names, purposes
  knownhosts  known_hosts files: patterns, hashed names, CAs, revocation,
            algorithm selection; agrees with ssh-keygen -F
  authkeys  authorized_keys files: from, expiry-time, cert-authority,
            principals, command, permitopen and the no- options
  krl       OpenSSH key revocation lists: keys, hashes, certificates by
            serial and key ID, CAs; agrees with ssh-keygen -Q
  agent     the messages of the ssh-agent protocol, for a client with its keys
            in an agent (the socket is the application's): framing,
            identities, signing, session-bind; checked against a real
            ssh-agent
  conn      the transport: negotiation, key exchange, rekeying, EXT_INFO,
            service request; interoperating with OpenSSH 9.6

  auth      user authentication (RFC 4252, RFC 4256): public key, also
            host-bound, password and keyboard-interactive, client and
            server; interoperating with OpenSSH 9.6

  chan      the connection protocol (RFC 4254): channels with strict flow
            control, session requests, TCP and Unix socket forwarding;
            interoperating with OpenSSH 9.6
  sftp      SFTP version 3, client and server, over a channel, with OpenSSH's
            extensions (posix-rename, statvfs, hardlink, fsync, lsetstat,
            limits, expand-path, copy-data, users-groups-by-id);
            interoperating with OpenSSH's sftp, scp and internal-sftp

  session   one object and one set of events over all of the above: routing,
            startup wiring, deadlines, policy defaults, SFTP binding

Start with include/foxtrot/session.h and the manual.  See the manual
(doc/foxtrot.texi) for the design, security policy and roadmap.

Building
--------

Requires libgcrypt >= 1.12.4 (earlier versions abort on some Ed25519 keys a
peer can send), and GnuTLS >= 3.8 for X.509 keys (optional).

  ./autogen.sh        # only when building from a repository checkout
  ./configure
  make
  make check

autogen.sh needs autoconf, automake, libtool and gettext (autopoint).
The build is expected to be free of compiler warnings.

Reporting bugs
--------------

Report bugs, including documentation bugs, to <dog@gnu.org>.

